{"v":"3.0.0","project":{"accession":"PRJDB45648","title":"Alzheimer's Disease Promotion by Ambient Fine Particulate Matter (PRJCA060651)","description":"This study investigates the causal mechanisms linking fine particulate matter (PM2.5) exposure to Alzheimer's disease (AD) pathogenesis via the gut-brain axis. Mechanistically, utilizing a real-world PM2.5 exposure 5xFAD mouse model, we demonstrate that PM2.5 accelerates cognitive impairment and β-amyloid deposition in a sex-dependent and disease-stage-specific manner. Fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) experiments confirm the mediating role of gut microbiota in PM2.5-induced neurotoxicity. Integrative multi-omics analysis of gut microbiota-derived extracellular vesicles (GMEVs) metaproteomics and brain spatial transcriptomics reveals that PM2.5-induced dysbiosis alters GMEV protein cargo, which targets astrocytic and neuronal populations in the hippocampus to promote amyloid-beta deposition and disrupt neural circuits. This research provides novel evidence for the early prevention of AD by elucidating how environmental pollutants trigger neuroimmune dysregulation through the microbiota-gut-brain axis.","organism":"unidentified","data_type":"Other","center_name":"Fudan University","submission_date":"2026/10/28 00:00","last_update":null},"sample_cols":["accession","title","collection_date","country","region","city","lat","lon","urban_status","urban_context","eligibility_reason","isolation_source","env_broad_scale","env_local_scale","env_medium","host","location_resolution","country_iso2","place_id","location_status","location_method","location_note","geo_loc_raw","collection_year"],"samples":[["SAMD01984294","AD3-198 (SAMC7358465)","2023-02-17",null,null,null,null,null,"non_environmental",null,"host-derived or clinical material",null,null,null,null,"Mus musculus","city",null,null,"unresolved",null,null,"missing: data agreement established pre-2023",2023],["SAMD01984295","AD3-187 (SAMC7358466)","2023-02-17",null,n